# Day 9 — Deploy a Linux Server on AWS

## Objective

The objective was to deploy a Linux server in AWS, configure the operating system, secure remote access using SSH, deploy a web service, and test connectivity.

## Architecture

```text
Windows PC
    |
    | SSH (TCP 22)
    v
AWS EC2 Instance
    |
    v
Amazon Linux 2023
    |
    v
Nginx Web Server
    |
    | HTTP (TCP 80)
    v
Web Browser

1. EC2 Instance Creation

An Amazon EC2 instance was created using Amazon Linux 2023.

The instance was successfully launched and reached the Running state.

Server Details
Operating System: Amazon Linux 2023
Architecture: x86_64
Default SSH user: ec2-user
Web server: Nginx
SSH Port: 22
HTTP Port: 80

2. Security Group Configuration

The EC2 Security Group was configured with the following inbound rules:

Type	Protocol	Port	Source
SSH	TCP	22	My IP
HTTP	TCP	80	0.0.0.0/0
Security Considerations

SSH access was restricted to my IP address instead of allowing SSH access from the entire Internet.

HTTP access was allowed from 0.0.0.0/0 because the purpose of the exercise was to make the web server accessible over the Internet.

3. SSH Connection

The EC2 instance was accessed remotely using SSH from Windows PowerShell.

The private key was secured using:

chmod 400 mylinuxKP.pem

The connection was successfully established using the EC2 key pair.

The first connection displayed the SSH host authenticity confirmation, after which the server was added to the local known_hosts file.

4. Verify Linux Server

After connecting to the server, the following commands were used.

Check Current User
whoami

Output:

ec2-user
Check Operating System
cat /etc/os-release

The server was confirmed to be running:
NAME="Amazon Linux"
VERSION="2023"
ID="amzn"
ID_LIKE="fedora"

uname -a
Linux ip-172-31-20-127.ec2.internal 6.18.51-120.162.amzn2023.x86_64 #2 SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Wed Sep 23 18:50:41 UTC 2026 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux
The server was confirmed to be running the Amazon Linux 2023 kernel on an x86_64 architecture.

Check Hostname
hostname

The server hostname was:
ip-172-31-20-127.ec2.internal

5. Update the Linux Server

The installed packages were checked and updated using:

sudo dnf update -y

The result was:

Dependencies resolved.
Nothing to do.
Complete!

This confirmed that there were no updates required at the time of the check.

6. Install Nginx

Nginx was installed using the Amazon Linux package manager:

sudo dnf install nginx -y

The installation was successful.

The installed Nginx version was verified using:

nginx -v

Output:

nginx version: nginx/1.30.5

7. Start Nginx
The Nginx service was started using:

sudo systemctl start nginx

Its status was then checked:

sudo systemctl status nginx

The service was confirmed to be:

Active: active (running)

The Nginx configuration was also successfully validated:

nginx: the configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf syntax is ok
nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test is successful
8. Enable Nginx at Boot

Nginx was configured to start automatically whenever the server boots:

sudo systemctl enable nginx

The command successfully created the required systemd symbolic link.

9. Test Nginx Locally

Nginx was tested from inside the EC2 instance using:

curl http://localhost

The command returned the Nginx Welcome page HTML.

This confirmed that:

Nginx is installed.
Nginx is running.
Nginx is listening for HTTP requests.
The local web server is responding correctly.
